  <h1>India’s Women Empowerment Policies — Progress and Gaps</h1>
  <div><em>The Hindu | Date: 2024 • ~600 words</em></div>
  <h3>Introduction</h3><p>Women’s empowerment remains central to India’s pursuit of inclusive growth and social justice. In 2024, The Hindu analysed progress in areas like representation, safety, and economic participation, noting achievements but also persistent inequalities. For UPSC, this is a cross-cutting theme relevant to society, governance, and polity.
________________________________________</p>
  <h3>Body</h3><div>________________________________________<br/>Introduction<br/><br/>Current Status<br/>•	Workforce Participation: Female Labour Force Participation Rate (FLFPR) ~28% (PLFS 2024).<br/>•	Representation: Women MPs ~15% in Lok Sabha (17th Lok Sabha).<br/>•	Education: Female literacy at 71.5% (NFHS-5).<br/>•	Health: Maternal Mortality Ratio (MMR) down to 97 per lakh live births (2020).<br/>•	Global Rank: India 127/146 in Global Gender Gap Index 2024.<br/>________________________________________<br/>Key Government Initiatives<br/>•	Legislative: Women’s Reservation Bill (2023) — 33% seats in Parliament/Assemblies (yet to be operationalised).<br/>•	Schemes:<br/>o	Beti Bachao Beti Padhao (BBBP).<br/>o	Pradhan Mantri Matru Vandana Yojana (PMMVY).<br/>o	Mahila Shakti Kendras.<br/>o	Nirbhaya Fund for women’s safety.<br/>•	Financial Inclusion: 23 crore women Jan Dhan accounts.<br/>•	Skill Development: Stand-Up India, Mudra loans for women entrepreneurs.<br/>________________________________________<br/>Achievements<br/>1.	Health Gains: Decline in MMR, improvement in institutional deliveries.<br/>2.	Education Access: Near gender parity in school enrolment.<br/>3.	Political Milestone: Passage of Women’s Reservation Bill.<br/>4.	Economic Participation: Rise of women-led startups, SHGs.<br/>5.	Awareness Growth: Media and campaigns mainstream gender equality.<br/>________________________________________<br/>Persistent Gaps<br/>1.	Low Workforce Participation: Cultural barriers, unpaid care work.<br/>2.	Safety Concerns: Crimes against women still high.<br/>3.	Wage Gap: Women earn ~30% less than men in similar jobs.<br/>4.	Leadership Deficit: Under-representation in bureaucracy, judiciary, corporates.<br/>5.	Intersectional Inequality: Dalit, tribal, rural women face compounded challenges.<br/>________________________________________<br/>Significance<br/>•	Social Justice: Women’s empowerment vital for equality.<br/>•	Economic Growth: Studies show FLFPR rise can boost GDP by 1–2%.<br/>•	Democracy: Women’s participation enhances inclusiveness.<br/>•	Human Development: Education, health, and rights interlinked.<br/>________________________________________<br/>UPSC Relevance<br/>•	GS-I (Society): Role of women, empowerment.<br/>•	GS-II (Governance): Welfare schemes, policies for women.<br/>•	GS-III (Economy): Employment, growth.<br/>•	Essay:<br/>o	“No nation can rise higher than the status of its women.”<br/>o	“Women’s empowerment is not charity, it is justice.”<br/>•	PSIR Paper II: Gender and politics.<br/>________________________________________<br/>Way Forward<br/>1.	Operationalise Women’s Reservation: Ensure quick implementation.<br/>2.	Expand Economic Opportunities: Childcare support, flexible work policies.<br/>3.	Strengthen Safety: Police reforms, fast-track courts, community programs.<br/>4.	Reduce Wage Gap: Equal pay enforcement.<br/>5.	Grassroots Empowerment: Strengthen SHGs and panchayat-level participation.<br/>________________________________________<br/>Conclusion</div>
  <h3>Conclusion</h3><p>India’s women empowerment journey shows progress in education, health, and politics, but deep gaps remain in economic participation and safety. True empowerment requires structural reforms that make equality a lived reality, not just a policy goal. For UPSC aspirants, it embodies the link between social justice, governance, and democracy.</p>
